Disclaimer of claims over the underlying tradition

ModelBrew AI makes no claim of ownership, authorship, or authority over:

  - the Navya-Nyaya tradition or any of its concepts, terminology, or
    classical texts;
  - the six-phase epistemic reasoning schema as formulated and described
    in the Sathish paper cited above;
  - the broader reasoning framework or any associated philosophical
    claims.

The Sanskrit terminology used in the schema (Samshaya, Pramana,
Pratyaksha, Anumana, Upamana, Shabda, Pancha Avayava, Pratijna, Hetu,
Udaharana, Upanaya, Nigamana, Tarka, Hetvabhasa, Savyabhichara,
Viruddha, Prakaranasama, Sadhyasama, Kalaatita, Nirnaya, and others)
comes from the Navya-Nyaya tradition of Indian logic and epistemology,
which is approximately one thousand years old and is in the public
domain. The specific modern formulation tested by this harness - the
particular six-phase ordering, the YAML frontmatter contract, the
Udaharana template phrasing, and the format and semantic scoring
criteria the paper proposes - is from Sathish 2026.

This project's contribution is strictly:

  1. a single-file Python test harness that issues the schema as a
     system prompt to frontier LLMs;
  2. a 10-problem benchmark set (3 verbatim from the paper, 7
     constructed in the same paper-described problem types);
  3. a format scorer and a keyword-group semantic scorer that report
     pass/fail and aggregate rates with Wilson 95% confidence
     intervals.

Nothing in this repository should be read as a claim of authorship over
the schema, the tradition, or the underlying ideas.
